## Context

The session records sit under every reporter and every reader. The session's own server appends the agent's reports; Claude Code's hook processes fire on every prompt and tool call, sometimes several at once; another session's start or end request may mark this one ended, and only a start check may clear that. The observer, the start check, the home list and the delete control all read the same records back and ask the same questions: is this session still open, and did any session of the project start after it?

Two identities meet here, and they do not line up one to one. Claude Code names the conversation and keeps that name when the user resumes it, even in a fresh process after exit or archive; each new process opens a new observer session, so one conversation can span several observer sessions, while a clear inside one process gives one observer session several Claude names. A join that replaces the previous one forgets the session the conversation continues. Logs also outlive the format: sessions recorded before pipelines were named must still read in full.

The records can also lie by omission. The end hook may be killed before it writes, leaving presence waiting and no mark for a session that no longer exists. Claude Code's own transcript is the one witness left: it closes with a record of its own, appends again on resume, and its format is undocumented and can run to tens of megabytes.

## Strategy

**One writer per file, three kinds of file.** The report log is appended only by its session's server; presence is replaced whole by each hook call, the newest hook time winning; an ended mark is created once, says whether the end hook, the user's approval or the transcript ended the session, and is removed only by a reopen or a delete. Keeping presence and marks out of the log means a hook firing mid-append can never split a line.

**The recorder stamps; one strict schema checks everywhere.** Time, session and order are stamped by the recorder, never supplied by the agent, and the same schema validates at append, at read and at the observer's intake. A line that fails is skipped alone. A line from before pipelines were named is valid and belongs to no pipeline: a missing name is history, not corruption.

**Record, then flag; never refuse.** Recording appends, then posts with every failure swallowed. When the session's conversation carries an ended mark of any kind, the reply says it was previously ended and to run the start check, so a stale conversation learns before it acts; a report alone clears nothing. An unreadable mark counts as none. In a fresh process the first report lands before the hook joins it to its conversation, so the flag rides on the next.

**The conversation is the identity; joins are added, never replaced.** Every report's reply names the observer session in a form the hook can read, and the hook stores it in the host session's presence. A resumed conversation keeps its Claude name but gets a new observer session, so the join adds it beside the earlier ones: everything ever joined is one conversation, and the session it continues stays findable. Unjoined presence still reads as an open session.

**One liveness rule, judged per conversation.** In order: an ended mark on any session of the conversation, then presence ended, then a transcript whose last record is the closing record for the same Claude session, then presence's last state, and with no presence the reported events. A conversation counts once, by its newest observer session, never as a second open session against itself. The halt check, home list and delete refusal all ask this one rule, and it writes nothing.

**The transcript only ever ends, from its own last record.** Only the final record counts, and only when it is the closing record of the conversation presence names, so a resume that writes on revives an unmarked session. A missing, unreadable or unrecognised transcript leaves the judgement exactly as it would be without it and never fails a read; quiet alone never ends anything. Reading only the transcript's end keeps it cheap enough for every judgement.

**Reopen, "newer" and delete each answer for the whole conversation.** Reopen removes the conversation's ended marks and nothing else, only when the start check asks; history before the end, while ended and after the reopen reads in one true order. "Newer" compares conversations, never one conversation's own processes, so a fresh process never makes a conversation newer than itself. Delete removes one ended session's log and mark, keeps presence another session still joins, and refuses a session whose conversation is live.

## Good examples

**Resumed in a fresh process, still the most recent.** The user exits at the plan gate and the end hook marks the session ended. Next morning they resume the conversation; the new process opens a new observer session, which the hook joins beside the earlier one. The next report is recorded and flagged previously ended; the start check finds no newer conversation, the mark is removed, and history runs across the end and the reopen in order.

**Archived without its end.** The user archives a session waiting on them; its end hook dies unwritten, so presence still says waiting and no mark exists. Its transcript's last record is the closing record for that same conversation, so every reader judges it closed without reporting its end, and judging writes nothing.

**Two hooks at once.** Two tool calls finish together and both hook processes replace the session's presence; the later hook time wins, and the report log is untouched.

## Bad examples

**A join that forgets.** The hook overwrites presence's link with the resumed process's new observer session. The earlier session's mark is out of reach, so the conversation is never told it was ended, and the earlier session, left with no presence, reads quiet and maybe stuck and halts its own continuation.

**An old closing record outlives a resume.** The judgement finds a closing record anywhere in the transcript, or one naming another conversation, so a resumed session building again still reads ended and a second session starts beside it.

**A report that refuses or reopens.** A report from an ended session is dropped, leaving a hole in its history; or recording clears the mark itself, so a conversation a newer session has since overtaken slips back in on a stale picture.

